
1. What the failure mode actually is
Name: Salience evasion / elephant blindness. The information needed to identify the most important issue is present in context, the model has the capability to identify it, and it still responds to the surface question instead.

Why it is "peculiar": it is not a knowledge failure, not a reasoning-capacity failure, and not a retrieval failure. It is a priority failure. The model can name the elephant if asked directly. It simply does not rank "name the elephant" as the operative task. That makes it invisible to benchmarks that test knowledge and capability, and visible only in the gap between what was asked and what mattered.

Operational definition for the estate:

An elephant is the highest-salience unresolved risk, contradiction, blocker, or vacuous control in the current context that, if left unaddressed, invalidates the response. Elephant blindness is any response that does not (a) name it, (b) rank it, and (c) either resolve it or explicitly block on it.

2. Map of the failure mode
2.1 Triggers
Trigger	Why it works
Literal-question framing	The user asks a narrow question; the model optimizes for that question and never asks "is this the right question?"
Smooth-context pressure	The surrounding text is confident and well-formed; the model continues the frame instead of interrupting it.
Sycophancy gradient	Naming the elephant creates friction; agreeing with the frame creates flow. Training rewards flow.
Long context	Salience dilutes with distance ("lost in the middle"); the loudest recent token wins over the most important earlier token.
Ceremonial controls	A guard, checklist, or "firewall" exists in the prompt or the pipeline; the model treats its presence as evidence of coverage.
Instruction-following narrowing	"Do X" narrows attention to X; the model does not scan for "what makes X unsafe/wrong/impossible."
2.2 Mechanisms
Next-token plausibility ≠ global salience. Generation optimizes local continuation of the frame, not global importance ranking.

No explicit salience step. Most pipelines go straight from context to answer, with no intermediate artifact that says "here is what matters most."

Reward mismatch. Helpfulness/agreement is rewarded; friction is penalized. The elephant is usually friction.

Control-plane theater. Guards that don't emit evidence are indistinguishable from guards that pass. The pipeline cannot tell "checked and clean" from "never checked."

Frame inheritance. The model adopts the user's framing as the task definition, rather than reconstructing the operative task from constraints, state, and risk.

2.3 Manifestations in the estate
Layer	How elephant blindness shows up
Founder surface	A confident answer to the question asked, while the blocking issue (missing prerequisite, contradiction, irreversible risk) is unmentioned.
Proof gate	verification_result: pass emitted by a verifier that never ran, or ran as a stub. (Your two scripts.)
Agent loop	Agent reports success on the stated subtask while the parent objective is unmet.
Retrieval	Agent retrieves what was asked for, not what would reveal the problem.
Override	Founder override is granted without recording the elephant the override is stepping over.
Living memory	The incident is logged as "resolved" because the response was smooth, not because the risk was addressed.
3. Why guards become elephants
The exit 0 stub is not an accident; it is the natural equilibrium of a control that is measured by presence rather than by evidence.

A guard fails safely into vacuity when:

Its success signal is "did not raise" rather than "produced a signed record of what it inspected and why it passed."

It is added to satisfy a checklist, not to block a named failure mode.

Nothing downstream distinguishes "ran and passed" from "did not run."

Its failure mode is not itself in the failure-mode register.

Rule: a control that cannot produce evidence of having run is not a control. It is decoration. Decoration that looks like a control is an elephant.

4. The reversal: the Elephant-Seeking Protocol (ESP)
The mindset shift is from "answer the question" to "find what makes the question dangerous, then answer." Architecturally, that means inserting a mandatory, evidence-emitting salience step before any founder-facing generation, and changing the objective function so that missing the elephant is the worst outcome — worse than being slow, worse than being blunt, worse than refusing.

4.1 Operational definition (first-class object)
Make the elephant a typed artifact, not a vibe:

text
ElephantRecord {
  elephant_id, task_id,
  kind: contradiction | blocker | vacuous_control | unverified_assumption | irreversible_risk | missing_prerequisite,
  description,
  evidence_refs,            # what in context proves it exists
  salience_score,           # ranked, not boolean
  disposition: resolved | blocked | escalated | overridden,
  override_ref?             # required if disposition == overridden
}
No founder-facing response is valid without either an empty ElephantRecord set with evidence of the scan, or a record whose top elephant has a disposition. "I looked and found nothing" must itself be evidenced.

4.2 Pre-response elephant scan (deterministic-first)
Before any generative call that will produce a founder-facing claim, run a structured scan. Most of it is machine-doable, which fits your existing deterministic gate:

Scan question	Machine-checkable?	Mechanism
Does the instruction contradict a known constraint?	Yes	Constraint checker against estate constraints
Does the plan require a resource/state that does not exist?	Yes	Dependency and state lookup (your 3-query surface)
Is any guard in the path a stub / non-evidence-emitting?	Yes	Static scan for exit 0, empty handlers, unregistered verifiers
Is the operative question different from the literal question?	Partly	Cheap classifier (Jev-style Choice) + explicit restatement
What is the strongest reason this response is wrong?	No	Bounded generative step, but required output, not optional
What is assumed but unverified?	Partly	Assumption extractor + proof lookup
The scan produces the ElephantRecord set. If the top elephant is consequential (irreversible, high-cost, high-blast-radius), the response is blocked or escalated, not softened.

4.3 Invert the objective function
State it explicitly in the agent contract:

Old objective: maximize plausibility and compliance with the apparent request.

New objective: minimize the probability that a consequential elephant is left unaddressed in a founder-facing output.

This changes the cost of errors. A smooth answer that misses the elephant is the worst outcome, not the best. A blunt answer that names it is the best outcome, even if it is less pleasant. This is the reward inversion that sycophancy training undoes; the estate has to re-impose it at the contract level, because the base model will not do it by default.

4.4 Make guards evidence-emitting (the anti-exit 0 rule)
Every control in the path must satisfy:

It emits a signed record of what it inspected, what it found, and why it passed or failed.

Absence of a record is a failure, not a pass. ProofUnavailable is a block, not a green light.

The record is replayable — same inputs, same verdict, verifiable offline.

The control's own failure mode is registered — "guard silently became a no-op" is a named incident class (this is your INC-08 class, applied to controls).

A firewall that cannot show what it blocked did not block anything.

4.5 Adversarial self-check as a required artifact
After the draft response exists and before it is released:

Generate the strongest counter-argument to the draft.

Check whether the counter-argument names an elephant not in the ElephantRecord.

If it does, the draft is revised or blocked; the record is updated.

This is cheap relative to a wrong founder-facing claim, and it is the mechanism that catches the elephant the first scan missed. It must be a required step with its own evidence artifact, not a prompt suggestion.

4.6 Reward, override, and memory
Reward: score responses by verified outcome and by elephant coverage, not by founder satisfaction. A response that blocks a bad path scores higher than one that enables it smoothly.

Override: a founder override must name the elephant it is overriding and carry a TTL. An override that does not reference an ElephantRecord is invalid. This prevents "override" from becoming the institutional way to ignore the elephant.

Living memory: every missed elephant becomes a regression case in the eval set. Every caught elephant becomes a positive example. The estate learns what not to miss, not just what to do.

4.7 Calibrated escalation
If the top elephant is consequential, the correct response is often not an answer but an escalation: to a verifier, to a human, or to a blocked state. The estate already has ProofUnavailable; add ElephantUnresolved as a first-class terminal state. An agent that can say "I will not answer until this is resolved" is more valuable than one that answers around it.

5. Concrete replacement for the two stubs
Your current scripts are the failure mode. Replace them with evidence-emitting controls, or delete them so they stop manufacturing trust.

trajectory_lock.py — should:

Take a trajectory ID and a claimed next action.

Verify the action is within the locked plan (hash of the approved trajectory).

Emit {trajectory_id, action_hash, allowed: bool, reason, signature} to the evidence log.

Exit non-zero when the action is out of plan. Never exit 0 unconditionally.

epistemic_firewall.py — should:

Take the draft response and the ElephantRecord set.

Check: every consequential elephant has a disposition; no vacuous guards in the path; no unverified assumption in a founder-facing claim.

Emit {draft_hash, elephant_ids, verdict, evidence_refs, signature}.

Exit non-zero and block release when the verdict is unresolved. Never exit 0 unconditionally.

Both should be registered in the failure-mode register with the explicit failure mode "silently becomes a no-op," and both should be replay-tested on a schedule to prove they still fire.

Also fix the shell hygiene: a # pasted into an interactive shell is a command, not a comment. The command not found was the shell telling you the same thing the stubs are telling you — the appearance of action is not action.

6. The one-line mindset shift
From: "What is the best answer to this question?"
To: "What is the thing in this context that, if I don't name it, makes my answer worthless — and have I produced evidence that I looked?"

Everything else in the ESP is machinery for making that question mandatory, evidenced, and replayable. The elephant is not found by trying harder to be helpful; it is found by making "did you check for the elephant, and can you prove it?" a gate that a vacuous exit 0 cannot pass.

